{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778976061547,"sessionID":"ses_1ccc3b105ffeGJVZ03NDbrWwnP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_yGzV9eTGoRjzMyOoGpzyeShi","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"jq -r '.title, \"---BODY---\", .body, \"---COMMENTS---\", (.comments[] | \"COMMENT by \\(.author.login):\\n\\(.body)\\n---\")' \"__issue_context__.json\"","timeout":120000,"workdir":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl","description":"Extracts full issue context JSON text"},"output":"[NTR] CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell / CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n---BODY---\nPlease check that the term does not already exist by using the ontology search tool OLS:\nhttps://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl\n\n\n**Preferred term label**\n- CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n- CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n\n**Synonyms** (add reference(s), please)\nCD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n- exhausted CD4 T cell\n- CD4+ exhausted T lymphocyte\n- exhausted CD4-positive T cell\n\nCD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n- exhausted CD8 T cell\n- CD8+ exhausted T lymphocyte\n- exhausted CD8-positive T cell\n\n**Definition** (free text, with reference(s), please. PubMed ID format is PMID:XXXXXX)\nCD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\nA CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation as a result of chronic antigenic stimulation(e.g., chronic infection, tumors, or persistent inflammation). This state is characterised by sustained expression of PD-1 as a shared feature. Additional exhaustion-associated molecules, including the transcription factor TOX, and context- or stage-dependent changes in markers such as LAG-3, TIM-3, CD39, and T-bet, may also be observed; however, the expression levels of these molecules vary depending on exhaustion stage and tissue environment.\n(PMID:31390978; PMID:31207603; PMID:36907685; PMID:38166256)\n\nCD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\nA CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation as a result of chronic antigenic stimulation (e.g., chronic infection, tumours, or persistent inflammation). This state is characterised by sustained expression of PD-1 as a shared feature. Additional exhaustion-associated molecules, including expression of the transcription factor TOX and reduced T-bet, and context- or stage-dependent changes in markers such as LAG-3, TIM-3, CD39, and EOMES, may also be observed; however, the expression levels of these molecules vary depending on exhaustion stage and disease context.\n(PMID:31207603; PMID:36907685; PMID:38166256)\n\n**Parent cell type term** (check the hierarchy here https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl)\nCD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n→ parent: CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell (CL_0000624)\n-> exhausted T cell **(Added by Caroline)**\n\nCD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n→ parent: CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell (CL_0000625)\n-> exhausted T cell **(Added by Caroline)**\n\n**Required markers**\n- expresses some 'PD-1 (PDCD1)'\n\n**Anatomical structure where the cell type is found** (check Uberon for anatomical structures: https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/uberon)\n\n\nNotes on placement:\nThe existing exhausted T cell (CL_0011025) is a lineage-free abstract class and should not be used as the parent.\nFollowing the existing structure of CL, lineage-defined subsets (memory, helper, naive) are placed under CD4-positive or CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell. Therefore, these exhausted subsets should also be placed directly under CD4-positive and CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell.\n\nKey references:\n- PMID:31390978 — Human CD4 exhausted T cells with sustained PD-1 expression.\n- PMID:31207603 — CD8 exhausted T cells showing persistent PD-1 and TOX-dependent programming.\n- PMID:36907685 — Exhausted T cell differentiation stages; PD-1 as the most consistent marker.\n- PMID:38166256 — Latest review summarizing exhaustion heterogeneity and PD-1 as universal hallmark.\n\n**Your ORCID**\n0000-0001-5742-4697\nhttps://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277\n\n**Additional notes or concerns**\n\n\n\n---COMMENTS---\nCOMMENT by scheuerm:\nRather than \"A CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation under chronic antigenic stimulation\" I would recommend \"A CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation **_as a result_** of chronic antigenic stimulation\"\n---\nCOMMENT by Caroline-99:\n**Initial trial using the agent mode - it delegated the task to CL-curator **  \n\nI've completed the initial research and validation for the two new exhausted T cell terms. Here's what I found:\n\n### ✅ Validated Information\n\n**Literature Review:**\n- Reviewed PMIDs 31390978, 31207603, 36907685, and 38166256\n- **PD-1 is confirmed as a universal hallmark** across exhausted T cells\n- **TOX is critical for CD8+ exhaustion** (PMID:31207603)\n- The comment by @scheuerm is correct - \\\"as a result of\\\" is more accurate (already in the definitions)\n\n**Parent Term Placement - APPROVED:**\n- CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell → CL:0000624 ✓\n- CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell → CL:0000625 ✓\n- Correctly NOT using CL:0011025 (exhausted T cell) as parent\n\n### ⚠️ Issue Identified\n\n**PMID:35880649 is INCORRECT** - This paper is about eye disease (nanophthalmos/retinitis pigmentosa), not T cell exhaustion. This reference should be removed or replaced.\n\n### 📦 Import Status\n\n**Required protein markers:**\n- ✅ PD-1 (PR:000001919) - already in CL\n- ✅ CD4, CD8, CD39, T-bet, EOMES - already in CL\n- 📥 LAG-3, TIM-3, TOX - added to pr_terms.txt for import\n\n### 🎯 Next Steps\n\n1. Need to run protein import refresh command\n2. Create the two new CL terms with:\n   - New IDs in CL_99xxxxx range (as per idrange requirements)\n   - Validated definitions (minus PMID:35880649)\n   - Logical definitions using parent terms and markers\n   - ORCID contributors added\n\n - Could you confirm if you want to proceed without PMID:35880649, or would you like to provide an alternative reference for epigenetic mechanisms in T cell exhaustion?\n\nSigned: GitHub Copilot\"\n---\nCOMMENT by Caroline-99:\n**A trial assigning directly to CL-curator** \n\n---\n\n# Curation Report: CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell & CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n\n**Issue**: #3453  \n**Date**: 6 January 2026  \n**Curator**: GitHub Copilot  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Term Identification\n\n**TWO TERMS REQUESTED:**\n\n### Term 1:\n- **Proposed Label**: CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n- **Status**: New term (NTR)\n- **Domain**: Cell Type - Immune Cell\n\n### Term 2:\n- **Proposed Label**: CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n- **Status**: New term (NTR)\n- **Domain**: Cell Type - Immune Cell\n\n---\n\n## 2. Definition Validation\n\n### CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n\n**Proposed Definition**: \n\nA CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation as a result of chronic antigenic stimulation (e.g., chronic infection, tumors, or persistent inflammation). This state is characterized by sustained expression of PD-1 as a shared feature. Additional exhaustion-associated molecules, including the transcription factor TOX, and context- or stage-dependent changes in markers such as LAG-3, TIM-3, CD39, and T-bet, may also be observed; however, the expression levels of these molecules vary depending on exhaustion stage and tissue environment.\n\n**Literature Support**:\n\n- **PMID:31390978** - Dong et al. (2019) - \"CD4+ T cell exhaustion revealed by high PD-1 and LAG-3 expression and the loss of helper T cell function in chronic hepatitis B.\" \n  - This paper directly demonstrates CD4+ T cell exhaustion in chronic HBV infection with high PD-1 and LAG-3 expression and impaired cytokine secretion (IFN-γ, IL-2, TNF-α).\n  - DOI: 10.1186/s12865-019-0309-9\n  - BMC Immunology, Open Access\n\n- **PMID:31207603** - Khan et al. (2019) - \"TOX transcriptionally and epigenetically programs CD8+ T cell exhaustion.\" \n  - While focused on CD8+ T cells, this landmark Nature paper established TOX as a central regulator of T cell exhaustion driven by calcineurin-NFAT2 signaling, critical for understanding exhaustion mechanisms applicable to both CD4+ and CD8+ subsets.\n  - DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1325-x\n  - Nature, Open Access\n\n- **PMID:36907685** - Zander & Cui (2023) - \"Exhausted CD8+ T cells face a developmental fork in the road.\" \n  - This review discusses exhausted T cell heterogeneity and highlights PD-1 as the most consistent marker across different stages.\n  - DOI: 10.1016/j.it.2023.02.006\n  - Trends in Immunology, Open Access via PMC\n\n- **PMID:38166256** - Baessler & Vignali (2024) - \"T Cell Exhaustion.\" \n  - Comprehensive review emphasizing that exhaustion results from chronic antigen stimulation and is characterized by sustained expression of multiple inhibitory receptors, altered transcriptome, epigenome, and metabolism.\n  - DOI: 10.1146/annurev-immunol-090222-110914\n  - Annual Review of Immunology\n\n**Validation Notes**:\n\nThe definition accurately reflects the current understanding of CD4+ exhausted T cells in the literature. The emphasis on PD-1 as a shared/universal marker is well-supported (PMID:31390978, PMID:38166256). The caveat about context- and stage-dependent expression of other markers (LAG-3, TIM-3, TOX, etc.) is appropriate given the heterogeneity documented in recent studies (PMID:36907685).\n\n**Structure**: The definition follows the CL definition guidelines by stating:\n1. The genus (CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell)\n2. The differentia (impaired function due to chronic antigenic stimulation)\n3. Key characteristics (PD-1 expression, variable expression of other markers)\n\n---\n\n### CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n\n**Proposed Definition**: \n\nA CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation as a result of chronic antigenic stimulation (e.g., chronic infection, tumors, or persistent inflammation). This state is characterized by sustained expression of PD-1 as a shared feature. Additional exhaustion-associated molecules, including expression of the transcription factor TOX and reduced T-bet, and context- or stage-dependent changes in markers such as LAG-3, TIM-3, CD39, and EOMES, may also be observed; however, the expression levels of these molecules vary depending on exhaustion stage and disease context.\n\n**Literature Support**:\n\n- **PMID:31207603** - Khan et al. (2019) - \"TOX transcriptionally and epigenetically programs CD8+ T cell exhaustion.\"\n  - Demonstrates that TOX is critical for CD8+ T cell exhaustion and operates via calcineurin-NFAT2 pathway. This is the primary reference establishing TOX's central role.\n  - DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1325-x\n  - Nature, Open Access\n\n- **PMID:36907685** - Zander & Cui (2023) - \"Exhausted CD8+ T cells face a developmental fork in the road.\"\n  - Reviews exhausted CD8+ T cell differentiation and heterogeneity, confirming PD-1 as the most consistent marker.\n  - DOI: 10.1016/j.it.2023.02.006\n  - Trends in Immunology, Open Access via PMC\n\n- **PMID:38166256** - Baessler & Vignali (2024) - \"T Cell Exhaustion.\"\n  - States exhaustion is characterized by \"poor proliferative potential; high inhibitory receptor expression; altered transcriptome, epigenome, and metabolism; and, most importantly, reduced effector function.\"\n  - DOI: 10.1146/annurev-immunol-090222-110914\n  - Annual Review of Immunology\n\n- **⚠️ PMID:35880649** - **INCORRECT REFERENCE** \n  - This paper is about ophthalmic genetics (MFRP variant causing nanophthalmos and retinitis pigmentosa), NOT about T cell exhaustion. \n  - **ACTION REQUIRED**: This reference must be removed or replaced with an appropriate paper on T cell exhaustion.\n\n**Validation Notes**:\n\nThe definition is scientifically accurate and well-supported. The distinction from CD4+ (mentioning \"reduced T-bet\" and \"EOMES\") is appropriate as these transcription factors have different roles in CD8+ T cells. \n\n**CRITICAL ISSUE**: PMID:35880649 is incorrectly cited - it's about eye disease, not T cell exhaustion. This reference must be removed. The three remaining references (PMID:31207603, 36907685, 38166256) provide excellent support for the term without this incorrect citation.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Cross-References\n\n### Primary References - CD4+ exhausted T cell:\n\n| PMID | DOI | Title | Relevance |\n|------|-----|-------|-----------|\n| 31390978 | 10.1186/s12865-019-0309-9 | CD4+ T cell exhaustion revealed by high PD-1 and LAG-3 expression... | PRIMARY source for CD4+ exhaustion |\n| 31207603 | 10.1038/s41586-019-1325-x | TOX transcriptionally and epigenetically programs CD8+ T cell exhaustion | Landmark paper on TOX mechanism |\n| 36907685 | 10.1016/j.it.2023.02.006 | Exhausted CD8+ T cells face a developmental fork in the road | Reviews exhaustion stages and markers |\n| 38166256 | 10.1146/annurev-immunol-090222-110914 | T Cell Exhaustion | Comprehensive review of mechanisms |\n\n### Primary References - CD8+ exhausted T cell:\n\nSame as above, with **PMID:35880649 requiring removal**.\n\n**RECOMMENDATION**: The four validated PMIDs (31390978, 31207603, 36907685, 38166256) provide excellent support for both terms. PMID:35880649 should be removed from the CD8+ term definition.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Parent Term Validation\n\n### CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n\n**Proposed Parent**: CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell (CL:0000624)\n\n**Justification**:\n\nThis parent is **APPROPRIATE and VALIDATED**. From cl-edit.owl:\n- CL:0000624 is defined as \"A mature alpha-beta T cell that expresses an alpha-beta T cell receptor and the CD4 coreceptor.\"\n- It has a logical definition: α-β T cell ∩ has_plasma_membrane_part(CD4) ∩ capable_of(some PR_000025402)\n- Existing hierarchy already places functional CD4+ T cell subsets under CL:0000624 (e.g., CL:0000492 - CD4+ helper T cell)\n\nThe requester's note is correct: the existing \"exhausted T cell\" (CL:0011025) is lineage-free and should not be the direct parent. Following CL patterns, lineage-specific exhausted subsets should be placed directly under their respective lineage parents.\n\n**Hierarchical Context**:\n```\nCL:0000791 (mature alpha-beta T cell)\n  ├─ CL:0000624 (CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell)\n  │   ├─ CL:0000492 (CD4-positive, alpha-beta helper T cell)\n  │   ├─ [existing memory/naive subsets]\n  │   └─ [NEW] CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n  └─ CL:0000625 (CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell)\n```\n\n---\n\n### CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n\n**Proposed Parent**: CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell (CL:0000625)\n\n**Justification**:\n\nThis parent is **APPROPRIATE and VALIDATED**. From cl-edit.owl:\n- CL:0000625 is defined as \"A T cell expressing an alpha-beta T cell receptor and the CD8 coreceptor.\"\n- Similar logical definition structure to CL:0000624\n- CL:0000624 and CL:0000625 are declared as disjoint classes (appropriately)\n\n**Hierarchical Context**:\n```\nCL:0000791 (mature alpha-beta T cell)\n  ├─ CL:0000624 (CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell)\n  └─ CL:0000625 (CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell)\n      └─ [NEW] CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n```\n\n**Additional Relationship**:\n\nBoth new terms should have a relationship to CL:0011025 (exhausted T cell). This could be captured via:\n- A logical axiom showing they are subtypes of the exhaustion phenotype\n- Or cross-reference annotations linking to the general exhausted T cell term\n\nThis maintains ontological coherence while respecting the lineage-based hierarchy.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Synonyms\n\n### CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n\n**Validated Synonyms**:\n| Synonym | Type | Source |\n|---------|------|--------|\n| exhausted CD4 T cell | EXACT | PMID:31390978 (used in title) |\n| CD4+ exhausted T lymphocyte | EXACT | Common nomenclature, PMID:41484095 |\n| exhausted CD4-positive T cell | EXACT | Standard variation |\n\n**Validation**: All proposed synonyms follow standard immunological nomenclature conventions. The variations (+/positive, T cell/T lymphocyte) are consistent with existing CL synonym patterns seen in CL:0000624.\n\n---\n\n### CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n\n**Validated Synonyms**:\n| Synonym | Type | Source |\n|---------|------|--------|\n| exhausted CD8 T cell | EXACT | PMID:31207603, PMID:36907685 |\n| CD8+ exhausted T lymphocyte | EXACT | Standard nomenclature, multiple papers |\n| exhausted CD8-positive T cell | EXACT | Standard variation |\n\n**Validation**: All synonyms are appropriate and follow CL conventions seen in CL:0000625.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Logical Relationships\n\n### Required Markers\n\n**PD-1 (PDCD1)** should be represented as a required marker:\n- Relationship: `has_plasma_membrane_part some PR:000025590` (or appropriate PR term for PD-1/PDCD1)\n- Justification: ALL cited papers emphasize PD-1 as the \"universal hallmark\" and \"most consistent marker\" of exhaustion\n\n### Functional Characteristics\n\nBased on the definitions emphasizing impaired function, consider relationships like:\n- `has_quality some PATO:decreased` (for impaired function)\n- Relationships to GO terms for decreased cytokine production\n- Relationships to GO terms for altered proliferation\n\nHowever, these should be carefully modeled according to existing CL patterns for functional characteristics.\n\n### Developmental/Classification Relationships\n\n**Relationship to existing exhausted T cell term**:\n- Both terms are more specific subtypes of CL:0011025 (exhausted T cell)\n- Could be captured via logical axioms or annotations\n- Maintains coherence: lineage-specific exhausted cells ARE exhausted T cells\n\n**Relationship to effector cells**:\n- CL:0011025 is currently: `SubClassOf(obo:CL_0011025 obo:CL_0000911)` [effector T cell]\n- The new terms inherit this relationship through their connection to exhaustion\n\n---\n\n## 7. Ontology Placement Recommendation\n\n### ✓ RECOMMENDED: Create in CL\n\n**Reasons:**\n\n1. **Within CL scope**: These are normal cell types that can exist physiologically (chronic infections) and pathologically (cancer). They represent functional states, not transformed cells.\n\n2. **Follows existing patterns**: CL already has CL:0011025 (exhausted T cell), and these are lineage-specific refinements following established CL hierarchy patterns.\n\n3. **Not pathological cells per se**: While associated with disease contexts, exhausted T cells are a functional differentiation state that occurs during normal immune responses to chronic antigens. They are not:\n   - Transformed/tumor cells\n   - Cultured/immortalized cell lines (not appropriate for CLO)\n   - Aberrant developmental states\n\n4. **Clinical and research utility**: \n   - Increasingly important for immunotherapy research\n   - Critical for checkpoint inhibitor therapy\n   - Essential for understanding chronic infections\n   - Needed for precise annotation of single-cell datasets\n\n5. **Precedent**: CL already contains other functional T cell states (memory, effector, naive, regulatory)\n\n**Integration Strategy:**\n- Place under lineage-specific parents (CL:0000624 and CL:0000625)\n- Add logical relationships to PD-1 expression\n- Add cross-references or logical relationships to CL:0011025\n- Follow existing CL patterns for functional T cell subsets\n\n---\n\n## 8. Additional Notes\n\n### Anatomical Location\n\nThe requester appropriately left this field blank. Exhausted T cells can be found in multiple anatomical locations depending on disease context:\n- **Tumor microenvironments** (various solid organs)\n- **Infected tissues** (e.g., liver in chronic HBV, lymph nodes in HIV)\n- **Peripheral blood**\n- **Secondary lymphoid organs**\n\nGiven this heterogeneity, specifying a single anatomical structure would be inappropriate and overly restrictive. The location depends on the source of chronic antigenic stimulation.\n\n---\n\n### Marker Expression Variability\n\nThe definitions appropriately include caveats that markers beyond PD-1 (LAG-3, TIM-3, TOX, T-bet, EOMES, CD39) vary by:\n\n1. **Stage of exhaustion**: \n   - Progenitor exhausted cells vs terminal exhausted cells\n   - Early exhaustion vs late exhaustion\n   \n2. **Disease context**: \n   - Chronic viral infection vs cancer\n   - Different tumor types\n   - Different pathogens\n   \n3. **Tissue environment**:\n   - Tumor microenvironment vs lymphoid organs\n   - Hypoxic vs normoxic regions\n\nThis nuance is well-supported by PMID:36907685 and PMID:38166256, and accurately reflects the current scientific understanding of exhaustion heterogeneity.\n\n---\n\n### TOX Expression\n\nTOX is mentioned as an exhaustion-associated molecule but not a defining requirement:\n- **Rationale**: While PMID:31207603 shows TOX is critical for establishing exhaustion programs, it operates upstream of sustained PD-1 expression\n- **PD-1 is more consistent**: Present across all stages and contexts\n- **TOX may be transient**: Important for programming but may not be maintained in all exhausted cells\n- **Context-dependent**: Expression levels vary by stage and tissue\n\nThis hierarchical relationship (TOX programs → sustained PD-1 expression) supports having PD-1 as the core defining feature.\n\n---\n\n### CD4+ vs CD8+ Distinctions\n\nThe definitions appropriately distinguish between the two subsets:\n\n**CD4+ exhausted cells**:\n- Emphasize loss of helper function (cytokine production)\n- Mention T-bet as a variable marker\n- Focus on helper cytokines (IFN-γ, IL-2, TNF-α)\n\n**CD8+ exhausted cells**:\n- Emphasize loss of cytotoxic function\n- Mention \"reduced T-bet\" and \"EOMES\" (distinct transcription factor profile)\n- Focus on cytotoxic capacity\n\nThese distinctions reflect genuine biological differences in how exhaustion manifests in helper vs cytotoxic lineages.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Confidence Assessment\n\n### CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n- **Definition**: ✅ High - well-supported by PMID:31390978 and recent reviews\n- **Parent term**: ✅ High - validated in CL hierarchy\n- **Cross-references**: ✅ High - all PMIDs validated and appropriate\n- **Synonyms**: ✅ High - follow CL conventions\n- **Overall**: **✅ HIGH CONFIDENCE**\n\n### CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n- **Definition**: ✅ High - well-supported by PMID:31207603 and comprehensive reviews\n- **Parent term**: ✅ High - validated in CL hierarchy\n- **Cross-references**: ⚠️ Medium - PMID:35880649 is INCORRECT and must be removed\n- **Synonyms**: ✅ High - follow CL conventions\n- **Overall**: **✅ HIGH CONFIDENCE** (pending removal of incorrect reference)\n\n---\n\n## 10. Critical Issues and Recommendations\n\n### ⚠️ CRITICAL ISSUE: Incorrect Reference\n\n**PMID:35880649 must be removed from CD8+ term**\n\nThis paper is about:\n- **Actual topic**: MFRP variant results in nanophthalmos, retinitis pigmentosa, variability in foveal avascular zone\n- **Journal**: Ophthalmic Genetics\n- **Subject**: Eye development and disease\n- **NOT related to**: T cells, exhaustion, or immunology\n\n**Recommendation**: Simply remove this reference. The three remaining validated references (PMID:31207603, 36907685, 38166256) provide excellent and sufficient support for the CD8+ exhausted T cell term.\n\n---\n\n### Required Metadata\n\n**ORCIDs to add as contributors**:\n- `terms:contributor <https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5742-4697>`\n- `terms:contributor <https://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277>`\n\n**Date stamps** (terms:date):\n- Add current timestamp in format: `\"2026-01-06T[time]Z\"^^xsd:dateTime`\n\n**Creator**:\n- Only add `terms:creator \"GitHub Copilot\"` if you are the primary creator of these NEW terms\n- Do NOT add creator annotation if only editing existing terms\n\n**Term tracker**:\n- Link back to issue: `term_tracker_item \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3453\"`\n\n---\n\n## 11. Summary and Final Recommendation\n\n### ✅ CURATION COMPLETE - READY FOR INTEGRATION\n\n**Summary:**\n- ✅ Two terms validated for CL integration\n- ✅ Definitions scientifically accurate and well-referenced\n- ✅ Parent terms appropriate and validated in CL hierarchy\n- ✅ Synonyms follow CL naming conventions\n- ✅ Markers (PD-1) well-supported as defining features\n- ⚠️ One reference issue identified and resolution provided\n\n**Action Items:**\n1. **Remove PMID:35880649** from CD8+ term definition citations\n2. **Add ORCID contributors** as specified\n3. **Add term tracker** link to issue #3453\n4. **Add date stamps** for creation date\n5. **Create terms** with IDs starting with CL_99xxxxx (per idrange guidelines)\n\n**Recommended Definition Citations (Final):**\n\n**Both terms should cite:**\n- PMID:31207603 (TOX mechanism - Nature paper)\n- PMID:36907685 (Exhaustion heterogeneity - Trends review)\n- PMID:38166256 (Comprehensive exhaustion review)\n\n**CD4+ term should additionally cite:**\n- PMID:31390978 (CD4+ exhaustion in HBV)\n\n**Next Steps:**\n\nPass to **@CL-ontologist** for:\n1. Integration into cl-edit.owl\n2. Assignment of new CL IDs (CL_99xxxxx range)\n3. Addition of appropriate logical axioms\n4. Quality control checks\n\n---\n\n## Appendix: Literature Summary\n\n### Key Papers Reviewed\n\n1. **PMID:31390978** (Dong et al., 2019) - BMC Immunology\n   - Direct evidence for CD4+ T cell exhaustion\n   - Shows high PD-1 and LAG-3 in chronic HBV\n   - Documents loss of cytokine production\n\n2. **PMID:31207603** (Khan et al., 2019) - Nature\n   - Landmark paper on TOX as master regulator\n   - Establishes epigenetic programming of exhaustion\n   - Shows calcineurin-NFAT2 upstream regulation\n\n3. **PMID:36907685** (Zander & Cui, 2023) - Trends in Immunology\n   - Reviews exhaustion heterogeneity\n   - Confirms PD-1 as most consistent marker\n   - Discusses progenitor vs terminal exhaustion\n\n4. **PMID:38166256** (Baessler & Vignali, 2024) - Annual Review of Immunology\n   - Comprehensive review of exhaustion mechanisms\n   - Covers transcriptional, epigenetic, metabolic changes\n   - Discusses therapeutic implications\n\n---\n\n**Report prepared by**: GitHub Copilot (CL-curator agent)  \n**Date**: 6 January 2026  \n**For issue**: #3453  \n**Status**: Ready for ontology integration\n\n---\n","metadata":{"output":"[NTR] CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell / CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n---BODY---\nPlease check that the term does not already exist by using the ontology search tool OLS:\nhttps://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl\n\n\n**Preferred term label**\n- CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n- CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n\n**Synonyms** (add reference(s), please)\nCD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n- exhausted CD4 T cell\n- CD4+ exhausted T lymphocyte\n- exhausted CD4-positive T cell\n\nCD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n- exhausted CD8 T cell\n- CD8+ exhausted T lymphocyte\n- exhausted CD8-positive T cell\n\n**Definition** (free text, with reference(s), please. PubMed ID format is PMID:XXXXXX)\nCD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\nA CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation as a result of chronic antigenic stimulation(e.g., chronic infection, tumors, or persistent inflammation). This state is characterised by sustained expression of PD-1 as a shared feature. Additional exhaustion-associated molecules, including the transcription factor TOX, and context- or stage-dependent changes in markers such as LAG-3, TIM-3, CD39, and T-bet, may also be observed; however, the expression levels of these molecules vary depending on exhaustion stage and tissue environment.\n(PMID:31390978; PMID:31207603; PMID:36907685; PMID:38166256)\n\nCD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\nA CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation as a result of chronic antigenic stimulation (e.g., chronic infection, tumours, or persistent inflammation). This state is characterised by sustained expression of PD-1 as a shared feature. Additional exhaustion-associated molecules, including expression of the transcription factor TOX and reduced T-bet, and context- or stage-dependent changes in markers such as LAG-3, TIM-3, CD39, and EOMES, may also be observed; however, the expression levels of these molecules vary depending on exhaustion stage and disease context.\n(PMID:31207603; PMID:36907685; PMID:38166256)\n\n**Parent cell type term** (check the hierarchy here https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl)\nCD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n→ parent: CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell (CL_0000624)\n-> exhausted T cell **(Added by Caroline)**\n\nCD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n→ parent: CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell (CL_0000625)\n-> exhausted T cell **(Added by Caroline)**\n\n**Required markers**\n- expresses some 'PD-1 (PDCD1)'\n\n**Anatomical structure where the cell type is found** (check Uberon for anatomical structures: https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/uberon)\n\n\nNotes on placement:\nThe existing exhausted T cell (CL_0011025) is a lineage-free abstract class and should not be used as the parent.\nFollowing the existing structure of CL, lineage-defined subsets (memory, helper, naive) are placed under CD4-positive or CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell. Therefore, these exhausted subsets should also be placed directly under CD4-positive and CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell.\n\nKey references:\n- PMID:31390978 — Human CD4 exhausted T cells with sustained PD-1 expression.\n- PMID:31207603 — CD8 exhausted T cells showing persistent PD-1 and TOX-dependent programming.\n- PMID:36907685 — Exhausted T cell differentiation stages; PD-1 as the most consistent marker.\n- PMID:38166256 — Latest review summarizing exhaustion heterogeneity and PD-1 as universal hallmark.\n\n**Your ORCID**\n0000-0001-5742-4697\nhttps://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277\n\n**Additional notes or concerns**\n\n\n\n---COMMENTS---\nCOMMENT by scheuerm:\nRather than \"A CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation under chronic antigenic stimulation\" I would recommend \"A CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation **_as a result_** of chronic antigenic stimulation\"\n---\nCOMMENT by Caroline-99:\n**Initial trial using the agent mode - it delegated the task to CL-curator **  \n\nI've completed the initial research and validation for the two new exhausted T cell terms. Here's what I found:\n\n### ✅ Validated Information\n\n**Literature Review:**\n- Reviewed PMIDs 31390978, 31207603, 36907685, and 38166256\n- **PD-1 is confirmed as a universal hallmark** across exhausted T cells\n- **TOX is critical for CD8+ exhaustion** (PMID:31207603)\n- The comment by @scheuerm is correct - \\\"as a result of\\\" is more accurate (already in the definitions)\n\n**Parent Term Placement - APPROVED:**\n- CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell → CL:0000624 ✓\n- CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell → CL:0000625 ✓\n- Correctly NOT using CL:0011025 (exhausted T cell) as parent\n\n### ⚠️ Issue Identified\n\n**PMID:35880649 is INCORRECT** - This paper is about eye disease (nanophthalmos/retinitis pigmentosa), not T cell exhaustion. This reference should be removed or replaced.\n\n### 📦 Import Status\n\n**Required protein markers:**\n- ✅ PD-1 (PR:000001919) - already in CL\n- ✅ CD4, CD8, CD39, T-bet, EOMES - already in CL\n- 📥 LAG-3, TIM-3, TOX - added to pr_terms.txt for import\n\n### 🎯 Next Steps\n\n1. Need to run protein import refresh command\n2. Create the two new CL terms with:\n   - New IDs in CL_99xxxxx range (as per idrange requirements)\n   - Validated definitions (minus PMID:35880649)\n   - Logical definitions using parent terms and markers\n   - ORCID contributors added\n\n - Could you confirm if you want to proceed without PMID:35880649, or would you like to provide an alternative reference for epigenetic mechanisms in T cell exhaustion?\n\nSigned: GitHub Copilot\"\n---\nCOMMENT by Caroline-99:\n**A trial assigning directly to CL-curator** \n\n---\n\n# Curation Report: CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell & CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n\n**Issue**: #3453  \n**Date**: 6 January 2026  \n**Curator**: GitHub Copilot  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Term Identification\n\n**TWO TERMS REQUESTED:**\n\n### Term 1:\n- **Proposed Label**: CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n- **Status**: New term (NTR)\n- **Domain**: Cell Type - Immune Cell\n\n### Term 2:\n- **Proposed Label**: CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n- **Status**: New term (NTR)\n- **Domain**: Cell Type - Immune Cell\n\n---\n\n## 2. Definition Validation\n\n### CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n\n**Proposed Definition**: \n\nA CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation as a result of chronic antigenic stimulation (e.g., chronic infection, tumors, or persistent inflammation). This state is characterized by sustained expression of PD-1 as a shared feature. Additional exhaustion-associated molecules, including the transcription factor TOX, and context- or stage-dependent changes in markers such as LAG-3, TIM-3, CD39, and T-bet, may also be observed; however, the expression levels of these molecules vary depending on exhaustion stage and tissue environment.\n\n**Literature Support**:\n\n- **PMID:31390978** - Dong et al. (2019) - \"CD4+ T cell exhaustion revealed by high PD-1 and LAG-3 expression and the loss of helper T cell function in chronic hepatitis B.\" \n  - This paper directly demonstrates CD4+ T cell exhaustion in chronic HBV infection with high PD-1 and LAG-3 expression and impaired cytokine secretion (IFN-γ, IL-2, TNF-α).\n  - DOI: 10.1186/s12865-019-0309-9\n  - BMC Immunology, Open Access\n\n- **PMID:31207603** - Khan et al. (2019) - \"TOX transcriptionally and epigenetically programs CD8+ T cell exhaustion.\" \n  - While focused on CD8+ T cells, this landmark Nature paper established TOX as a central regulator of T cell exhaustion driven by calcineurin-NFAT2 signaling, critical for understanding exhaustion mechanisms applicable to both CD4+ and CD8+ subsets.\n  - DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1325-x\n  - Nature, Open Access\n\n- **PMID:36907685** - Zander & Cui (2023) - \"Exhausted CD8+ T cells face a developmental fork in the road.\" \n  - This review discusses exhausted T cell heterogeneity and highlights PD-1 as the most consistent marker across different stages.\n  - DOI: 10.1016/j.it.2023.02.006\n  - Trends in Immunology, Open Access via PMC\n\n- **PMID:38166256** - Baessler & Vignali (2024) - \"T Cell Exhaustion.\" \n  - Comprehensive review emphasizing that exhaustion results from chronic antigen stimulation and is characterized by sustained expression of multiple inhibitory receptors, altered transcriptome, epigenome, and metabolism.\n  - DOI: 10.1146/annurev-immunol-090222-110914\n  - Annual Review of Immunology\n\n**Validation Notes**:\n\nThe definition accurately reflects the current understanding of CD4+ exhausted T cells in the literature. The emphasis on PD-1 as a shared/universal marker is well-supported (PMID:31390978, PMID:38166256). The caveat about context- and stage-dependent expression of other markers (LAG-3, TIM-3, TOX, etc.) is appropriate given the heterogeneity documented in recent studies (PMID:36907685).\n\n**Structure**: The definition follows the CL definition guidelines by stating:\n1. The genus (CD4-positive alpha-beta T cell)\n2. The differentia (impaired function due to chronic antigenic stimulation)\n3. Key characteristics (PD-1 expression, variable expression of other markers)\n\n---\n\n### CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n\n**Proposed Definition**: \n\nA CD8-positive alpha-beta T cell that displays impaired function and altered differentiation as a result of chronic antigenic stimulation (e.g., chronic infection, tumors, or persistent inflammation). This state is characterized by sustained expression of PD-1 as a shared feature. Additional exhaustion-associated molecules, including expression of the transcription factor TOX and reduced T-bet, and context- or stage-dependent changes in markers such as LAG-3, TIM-3, CD39, and EOMES, may also be observed; however, the expression levels of these molecules vary depending on exhaustion stage and disease context.\n\n**Literature Support**:\n\n- **PMID:31207603** - Khan et al. (2019) - \"TOX transcriptionally and epigenetically programs CD8+ T cell exhaustion.\"\n  - Demonstrates that TOX is critical for CD8+ T cell exhaustion and operates via calcineurin-NFAT2 pathway. This is the primary reference establishing TOX's central role.\n  - DOI: 10.1038/s41586-019-1325-x\n  - Nature, Open Access\n\n- **PMID:36907685** - Zander & Cui (2023) - \"Exhausted CD8+ T cells face a developmental fork in the road.\"\n  - Reviews exhausted CD8+ T cell differentiation and heterogeneity, confirming PD-1 as the most consistent marker.\n  - DOI: 10.1016/j.it.2023.02.006\n  - Trends in Immunology, Open Access via PMC\n\n- **PMID:38166256** - Baessler & Vignali (2024) - \"T Cell Exhaustion.\"\n  - States exhaustion is characterized by \"poor proliferative potential; high inhibitory receptor expression; altered transcriptome, epigenome, and metabolism; and, most importantly, reduced effector function.\"\n  - DOI: 10.1146/annurev-immunol-090222-110914\n  - Annual Review of Immunology\n\n- **⚠️ PMID:35880649** - **INCORRECT REFERENCE** \n  - This paper is about ophthalmic genetics (MFRP variant causing nanophthalmos and retinitis pigmentosa), NOT about T cell exhaustion. \n  - **ACTION REQUIRED**: This reference must be removed or replaced with an appropriate paper on T cell exhaustion.\n\n**Validation Notes**:\n\nThe definition is scientifically accurate and well-supported. The distinction from CD4+ (mentioning \"reduced T-bet\" and \"EOMES\") is appropriate as these transcription factors have different roles in CD8+ T cells. \n\n**CRITICAL ISSUE**: PMID:35880649 is incorrectly cited - it's about eye disease, not T cell exhaustion. This reference must be removed. The three remaining references (PMID:31207603, 36907685, 38166256) provide excellent support for the term without this incorrect citation.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Cross-References\n\n### Primary References - CD4+ exhausted T cell:\n\n| PMID | DOI | Title | Relevance |\n|------|-----|-------|-----------|\n| 31390978 | 10.1186/s12865-019-0309-9 | CD4+ T cell exhaustion revealed by high PD-1 and LAG-3 expression... | PRIMARY source for CD4+ exhaustion |\n| 31207603 | 10.1038/s41586-019-1325-x | TOX transcriptionally and epigenetically programs CD8+ T cell exhaustion | Landmark paper on TOX mechanism |\n| 36907685 | 10.1016/j.it.2023.02.006 | Exhausted CD8+ T cells face a developmental fork in the road | Reviews exhaustion stages and markers |\n| 38166256 | 10.1146/annurev-immunol-090222-110914 | T Cell Exhaustion | Comprehensive review of mechanisms |\n\n### Primary References - CD8+ exhausted T cell:\n\nSame as above, with **PMID:35880649 requiring removal**.\n\n**RECOMMENDATION**: The four validated PMIDs (31390978, 31207603, 36907685, 38166256) provide excellent support for both terms. PMID:35880649 should be removed from the CD8+ term definition.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Parent Term Validation\n\n### CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n\n**Proposed Parent**: CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell (CL:0000624)\n\n**Justification**:\n\nThis parent is **APPROPRIATE and VALIDATED**. From cl-edit.owl:\n- CL:0000624 is defined as \"A mature alpha-beta T cell that expresses an alpha-beta T cell receptor and the CD4 coreceptor.\"\n- It has a logical definition: α-β T cell ∩ has_plasma_membrane_part(CD4) ∩ capable_of(some PR_000025402)\n- Existing hierarchy already places functional CD4+ T cell subsets under CL:0000624 (e.g., CL:0000492 - CD4+ helper T cell)\n\nThe requester's note is correct: the existing \"exhausted T cell\" (CL:0011025) is lineage-free and should not be the direct parent. Following CL patterns, lineage-specific exhausted subsets should be placed directly under their respective lineage parents.\n\n**Hierarchical Context**:\n```\nCL:0000791 (mature alpha-beta T cell)\n  ├─ CL:0000624 (CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell)\n  │   ├─ CL:0000492 (CD4-positive, alpha-beta helper T cell)\n  │   ├─ [existing memory/naive subsets]\n  │   └─ [NEW] CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n  └─ CL:0000625 (CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell)\n```\n\n---\n\n### CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n\n**Proposed Parent**: CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell (CL:0000625)\n\n**Justification**:\n\nThis parent is **APPROPRIATE and VALIDATED**. From cl-edit.owl:\n- CL:0000625 is defined as \"A T cell expressing an alpha-beta T cell receptor and the CD8 coreceptor.\"\n- Similar logical definition structure to CL:0000624\n- CL:0000624 and CL:0000625 are declared as disjoint classes (appropriately)\n\n**Hierarchical Context**:\n```\nCL:0000791 (mature alpha-beta T cell)\n  ├─ CL:0000624 (CD4-positive, alpha-beta T cell)\n  └─ CL:0000625 (CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell)\n      └─ [NEW] CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell\n```\n\n**Additional Relationship**:\n\nBoth new terms should have a relationship to CL:0011025 (exhausted T cell). This could be captured via:\n- A logical axiom showing they are subtypes of the exhaustion phenotype\n- Or cross-reference annotations linking to the general exhausted T cell term\n\nThis maintains ontological coherence while respecting the lineage-based hierarchy.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Synonyms\n\n### CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n\n**Validated Synonyms**:\n| Synonym | Type | Source |\n|---------|------|--------|\n| exhausted CD4 T cell | EXACT | PMID:31390978 (used in title) |\n| CD4+ exhausted T lymphocyte | EXACT | Common nomenclature, PMID:41484095 |\n| exhausted CD4-positive T cell | EXACT | Standard variation |\n\n**Validation**: All proposed synonyms follow standard immunological nomenclature conventions. The variations (+/positive, T cell/T lymphocyte) are consistent with existing CL synonym patterns seen in CL:0000624.\n\n---\n\n### CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n\n**Validated Synonyms**:\n| Synonym | Type | Source |\n|---------|------|--------|\n| exhausted CD8 T cell | EXACT | PMID:31207603, PMID:36907685 |\n| CD8+ exhausted T lymphocyte | EXACT | Standard nomenclature, multiple papers |\n| exhausted CD8-positive T cell | EXACT | Standard variation |\n\n**Validation**: All synonyms are appropriate and follow CL conventions seen in CL:0000625.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Logical Relationships\n\n### Required Markers\n\n**PD-1 (PDCD1)** should be represented as a required marker:\n- Relationship: `has_plasma_membrane_part some PR:000025590` (or appropriate PR term for PD-1/PDCD1)\n- Justification: ALL cited papers emphasize PD-1 as the \"universal hallmark\" and \"most consistent marker\" of exhaustion\n\n### Functional Characteristics\n\nBased on the definitions emphasizing impaired function, consider relationships like:\n- `has_quality some PATO:decreased` (for impaired function)\n- Relationships to GO terms for decreased cytokine production\n- Relationships to GO terms for altered proliferation\n\nHowever, these should be carefully modeled according to existing CL patterns for functional characteristics.\n\n### Developmental/Classification Relationships\n\n**Relationship to existing exhausted T cell term**:\n- Both terms are more specific subtypes of CL:0011025 (exhausted T cell)\n- Could be captured via logical axioms or annotations\n- Maintains coherence: lineage-specific exhausted cells ARE exhausted T cells\n\n**Relationship to effector cells**:\n- CL:0011025 is currently: `SubClassOf(obo:CL_0011025 obo:CL_0000911)` [effector T cell]\n- The new terms inherit this relationship through their connection to exhaustion\n\n---\n\n## 7. Ontology Placement Recommendation\n\n### ✓ RECOMMENDED: Create in CL\n\n**Reasons:**\n\n1. **Within CL scope**: These are normal cell types that can exist physiologically (chronic infections) and pathologically (cancer). They represent functional states, not transformed cells.\n\n2. **Follows existing patterns**: CL already has CL:0011025 (exhausted T cell), and these are lineage-specific refinements following established CL hierarchy patterns.\n\n3. **Not pathological cells per se**: While associated with disease contexts, exhausted T cells are a functional differentiation state that occurs during normal immune responses to chronic antigens. They are not:\n   - Transformed/tumor cells\n   - Cultured/immortalized cell lines (not appropriate for CLO)\n   - Aberrant developmental states\n\n4. **Clinical and research utility**: \n   - Increasingly important for immunotherapy research\n   - Critical for checkpoint inhibitor therapy\n   - Essential for understanding chronic infections\n   - Needed for precise annotation of single-cell datasets\n\n5. **Precedent**: CL already contains other functional T cell states (memory, effector, naive, regulatory)\n\n**Integration Strategy:**\n- Place under lineage-specific parents (CL:0000624 and CL:0000625)\n- Add logical relationships to PD-1 expression\n- Add cross-references or logical relationships to CL:0011025\n- Follow existing CL patterns for functional T cell subsets\n\n---\n\n## 8. Additional Notes\n\n### Anatomical Location\n\nThe requester appropriately left this field blank. Exhausted T cells can be found in multiple anatomical locations depending on disease context:\n- **Tumor microenvironments** (various solid organs)\n- **Infected tissues** (e.g., liver in chronic HBV, lymph nodes in HIV)\n- **Peripheral blood**\n- **Secondary lymphoid organs**\n\nGiven this heterogeneity, specifying a single anatomical structure would be inappropriate and overly restrictive. The location depends on the source of chronic antigenic stimulation.\n\n---\n\n### Marker Expression Variability\n\nThe definitions appropriately include caveats that markers beyond PD-1 (LAG-3, TIM-3, TOX, T-bet, EOMES, CD39) vary by:\n\n1. **Stage of exhaustion**: \n   - Progenitor exhausted cells vs terminal exhausted cells\n   - Early exhaustion vs late exhaustion\n   \n2. **Disease context**: \n   - Chronic viral infection vs cancer\n   - Different tumor types\n   - Different pathogens\n   \n3. **Tissue environment**:\n   - Tumor microenvironment vs lymphoid organs\n   - Hypoxic vs normoxic regions\n\nThis nuance is well-supported by PMID:36907685 and PMID:38166256, and accurately reflects the current scientific understanding of exhaustion heterogeneity.\n\n---\n\n### TOX Expression\n\nTOX is mentioned as an exhaustion-associated molecule but not a defining requirement:\n- **Rationale**: While PMID:31207603 shows TOX is critical for establishing exhaustion programs, it operates upstream of sustained PD-1 expression\n- **PD-1 is more consistent**: Present across all stages and contexts\n- **TOX may be transient**: Important for programming but may not be maintained in all exhausted cells\n- **Context-dependent**: Expression levels vary by stage and tissue\n\nThis hierarchical relationship (TOX programs → sustained PD-1 expression) supports having PD-1 as the core defining feature.\n\n---\n\n### CD4+ vs CD8+ Distinctions\n\nThe definitions appropriately distinguish between the two subsets:\n\n**CD4+ exhausted cells**:\n- Emphasize loss of helper function (cytokine production)\n- Mention T-bet as a variable marker\n- Focus on helper cytokines (IFN-γ, IL-2, TNF-α)\n\n**CD8+ exhausted cells**:\n- Emphasize loss of cytotoxic function\n- Mention \"reduced T-bet\" and \"EOMES\" (distinct transcription factor profile)\n- Focus on cytotoxic capacity\n\nThese distinctions reflect genuine biological differences in how exhaustion manifests in helper vs cytotoxic lineages.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Confidence Assessment\n\n### CD4-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n- **Definition**: ✅ High - well-supported by PMID:31390978 and recent reviews\n- **Parent term**: ✅ High - validated in CL hierarchy\n- **Cross-references**: ✅ High - all PMIDs validated and appropriate\n- **Synonyms**: ✅ High - follow CL conventions\n- **Overall**: **✅ HIGH CONFIDENCE**\n\n### CD8-positive exhausted alpha-beta T cell:\n- **Definition**: ✅ High - well-supported by PMID:31207603 and comprehensive reviews\n- **Parent term**: ✅ High - validated in CL hierarchy\n- **Cross-references**: ⚠️ Medium - PMID:35880649 is INCORRECT and must be removed\n- **Synonyms**: ✅ High - follow CL conventions\n- **Overall**: **✅ HIGH CONFIDENCE** (pending removal of incorrect reference)\n\n---\n\n## 10. Critical Issues and Recommendations\n\n### ⚠️ CRITICAL ISSUE: Incorrect Reference\n\n**PMID:35880649 must be removed from CD8+ term**\n\nThis paper is about:\n- **Actual topic**: MFRP variant results in nanophthalmos, retinitis pigmentosa, variability in foveal avascular zone\n- **Journal**: Ophthalmic Genetics\n- **Subject**: Eye development and disease\n- **NOT related to**: T cells, exhaustion, or immunology\n\n**Recommendation**: Simply remove this reference. The three remaining validated references (PMID:31207603, 36907685, 38166256) provide excellent and sufficient support for the CD8+ exhausted T cell term.\n\n---\n\n### Required Metadata\n\n**ORCIDs to add as contributors**:\n- `terms:contributor <https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5742-4697>`\n- `terms:contributor <https://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277>`\n\n**Date stamps** (terms:date):\n- Add current timestamp in format: `\"2026-01-06T[time]Z\"^^xsd:dateTime`\n\n**Creator**:\n- Only add `terms:creator \"GitHub Copilot\"` if you are the primary creator of these NEW terms\n- Do NOT add creator annotation if only editing existing terms\n\n**Term tracker**:\n- Link back to issue: `term_tracker_item \"https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/issues/3453\"`\n\n---\n\n## 11. Summary and Final Recommendation\n\n### ✅ CURATION COMPLETE - READY FOR INTEGRATION\n\n**Summary:**\n- ✅ Two terms validated for CL integration\n- ✅ Definitions scientifically accurate and well-referenced\n- ✅ Parent terms appropriate and validated in CL hierarchy\n- ✅ Synonyms follow CL naming conventions\n- ✅ Markers (PD-1) well-supported as defining features\n- ⚠️ One reference issue identified and resolution provided\n\n**Action Items:**\n1. **Remove PMID:35880649** from CD8+ term definition citations\n2. **Add ORCID contributors** as specified\n3. **Add term tracker** link to issue #3453\n4. **Add date stamps** for creation date\n5. **Create terms** with IDs starting with CL_99xxxxx (per idrange guidelines)\n\n**Recommended Definition Citations (Final):**\n\n**Both terms should cite:**\n- PMID:31207603 (TOX mechanism - Nature paper)\n- PMID:36907685 (Exhaustion heterogeneity - Trends review)\n- PMID:38166256 (Comprehensive exhaustion review)\n\n**CD4+ term should additionally cite:**\n- PMID:31390978 (CD4+ exhaustion in HBV)\n\n**Next Steps:**\n\nPass to **@CL-ontologist** for:\n1. Integration into cl-edit.owl\n2. Assignment of new CL IDs (CL_99xxxxx range)\n3. Addition of appropriate logical axioms\n4. Quality control checks\n\n---\n\n## Appendix: Literature Summary\n\n### Key Papers Reviewed\n\n1. **PMID:31390978** (Dong et al., 2019) - BMC Immunology\n   - Direct evidence for CD4+ T cell exhaustion\n   - Shows high PD-1 and LAG-3 in chronic HBV\n   - Documents loss of cytokine production\n\n2. **PMID:31207603** (Khan et al., 2019) - Nature\n   - Landmark paper on TOX as master regulator\n   - Establishes epigenetic programming of exhaustion\n   - Shows calcineurin-NFAT2 upstream regulation\n\n3. **PMID:36907685** (Zander & Cui, 2023) - Trends in Immunology\n   - Reviews exhaustion heterogeneity\n   - Confirms PD-1 as most consistent marker\n   - Discusses progenitor vs terminal exhaustion\n\n4. **PMID:38166256** (Baessler & Vignali, 2024) - Annual Review of Immunology\n   - Comprehensive review of exhaustion mechanisms\n   - Covers transcriptional, epigenetic, metabolic changes\n   - Discusses therapeutic implications\n\n---\n\n**Report prepared by**: GitHub Copilot (CL-curator agent)  \n**Date**: 6 January 2026  \n**For issue**: #3453  \n**Status**: Ready for ontology integration\n\n---\n","exit":0,"description":"Extracts full issue context JSON text","truncated":false},"title":"Extracts full issue context JSON text","time":{"start":1778976061528,"end":1778976061535}},"id":"prt_e333c71bb001KFJrMGrKOzWiX7","sessionID":"ses_1ccc3b105ffeGJVZ03NDbrWwnP","messageID":"msg_e333c62df001ZN5sAoAdiEiak9"}}
